-- NEGATIVE fixture for the future D-CYCLE check (spec §6.4.2, §6.3.7), and THE
-- WORST INSTANCE IN THE SET.
--
-- p8: the cycle runs through a GUARD, i.e. through a table's INPUT position
-- rather than through an output expression.
--
--     `the relief applies`  MEANS BRANCH IF `the charge is due` THEN FALSE
--                                        OTHERWISE TRUE
--     `the charge is due`   MEANS BRANCH IF `the relief applies` THEN FALSE
--                                        OTHERWISE TRUE
--
-- Why it is the worst: the exporter's fidelity report reads `(nothing lost)` on
-- this shape. Both decisions tabulate cleanly, every guard renders, no note
-- fires -- and the file it certifies is one all three engines reject (§6.4.3(a):
-- KIE reports `Cyclic dependency detected` then SKIPPED, Camunda 7 refuses with
-- `DMN-02015 ... has a loop`, Camunda 8 rejects at parse with `Cyclic
-- dependencies between decisions detected`). An exporter that says nothing lost
-- about an unloadable artifact is worse than one that refuses, because the
-- refusal is at least legible.
--
-- Expected after Phase 4: one D-CYCLE over an SCC of size 2, and `(nothing
-- lost)` must no longer be the report.
--
--
-- PROVENANCE OF THIS HEADER. The .l4 source was typechecked (`l4 check`, clean)
-- when it was written on 2026-07-31, with a prebuilt CLI. The statements above
-- about what TODAY'S EXPORTER emits and reports are taken from spec
-- §6.4.2/§6.4.5's table and were NOT re-measured here: that session had no
-- build. Whoever wires these fixtures into jl4/tests/DmnExport.hs must MEASURE
-- the expectation rather than transcribe it.
-- NEVER added to `goldenSubjects`.

IMPORT prelude

§ `cycle through a guard`

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the relief applies` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the charge is due` THEN FALSE
           OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the charge is due` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the relief applies` THEN FALSE
           OTHERWISE TRUE
